[stderr] # 10 — Specification-to-runbook authoring handoff
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Purpose:** hand the audited planning catalogue to F011–F016 without starting
[stderr] product implementation, testing, deployment, or release activity. This file is
[stderr] an authoring control record, not an implementation plan that overrides the
[stderr] [source brief](../INITIAL.md).
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Authoritative inputs and evidence locations
[stderr] 
[stderr] Read these in order. If sources conflict, preserve the source brief, record the
[stderr] conflict in the F011 decision log, and stop the affected allocation rather than
[stderr] inventing a resolution.
[stderr] 
[stderr] 1. [INITIAL.md](../INITIAL.md) is the original source of truth; the normalized
[stderr]    [product brief](../specification/01-product-brief.md) preserves its scope.
[stderr] 2. [Domain discovery](../specification/00-domain-discovery.md), the feature,
[stderr]    technical, and experience contracts (`02`–`04`) define the discovered
[stderr]    product and target constraints.
[stderr] 3. [Delivery map](05-delivery-map.md) provides the dependency order DM-01 to
[stderr]    DM-20. [Foundation](06-foundation-domain-contracts.md),
[stderr]    [feature](07-feature-experience-contracts.md), and
[stderr]    [quality](08-quality-operations-contracts.md) define FDN-01–09,
[stderr]    FEX-01–14, and QOR-01–08 ownership and acceptance evidence.
[stderr] 4. [Contract audit](09-contract-audit.md) is the current closure evidence,
[stderr]    including browser-spec ownership and retained decisions.
[stderr] 
[stderr] F011 writes canonical specification and traceability evidence under
[stderr] `specification/` (including `requirements.md`, `requirements.json`, a
[stderr] traceability matrix, decision log, platform appendix, and specification index).
[stderr] F012 writes the implementation catalogue and graph under `planning/`; F013
[stderr] writes the batch manifest there; F014 writes B-series authoring runbooks under
[stderr] `authoring-runbooks/` and their registration records; F015 writes structural
[stderr] quality-gate evidence; and F016 writes the project README and durable resume
[stderr] handoff. Generated R-series instructions and their evidence belong in
[stderr] `runbooks/` and the locations named by their final contracts. Planned test and
[stderr] browser paths are ownership reservations only until a later implementation task
[stderr] creates executable code and independent evidence.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Scope boundary and original-design rule
[stderr] 
[stderr] The P0 to author is an original, offline-capable 2D, single-player fantasy RPG:
[stderr] one town, one dungeon, a guided short campaign ending, exploration, dialogue,
[stderr] quests, party-based turn-based combat, inventory/rewards, onboarding, local
[stderr] save/load/checkpoints/recovery, settings/accessibility, responsive web, and an
[stderr] installable PWA with safe offline/update behaviour. It uses a shared core with
[stderr] only necessary web/PWA adaptations.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Do not author implementation work for additional regions, chapters, native
[stderr] apps, PWA-exclusive rules, live events, player-created/shared content, advanced
[stderr] analytics/monetisation, cloud sync, payment flows, accounts/identity,
[stderr] multiplayer, moderation, player-safety services, live operations, or
[stderr] entitlements. The brief mentions several connected capabilities, but their
[stderr] conflict with the offline P0 remains an explicit hold; no author may recast
[stderr] that hold as approved scope.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The Final Fantasy V reference permits only high-level party-based turn-based
[stderr] adventure scope. Never reproduce or request copied names, branding, assets,
[stderr] story/lore, dialogue, maps, music, layouts, menus, combat screens, interaction
[stderr] choreography, or distinctive presentation. All planned content and assets must
[stderr] be independently original, warm hand-painted fantasy with readable silhouettes
[stderr] and a restrained palette, supported by provenance records and later human
[stderr] review.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Execution order and dependencies
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Stage | Required outcome | Inputs / dependency | Cannot proceed without |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | F011 | Canonical `REQ-*`, `NFR-*`, `DEC-*`, `RISK-*`, `GATE-*` system and traceability | This handoff and all specifications/planning 05–09 | Recording, not deciding, open choices |
[stderr] | F012 | Bounded `IMP-*` catalogue, ownership, and acyclic graph | Completed F011 | Requirement traceability and named domain shards |
[stderr] | F013 | Persistent B/R allocation ledger and context packets | Completed F012 | Unique immutable IDs and dependency-safe batches of at most seven R contracts |
[stderr] | F014 | Bootstrap B-series/dispatcher collection and child registrations | Completed F013 | Valid manifest, bounded wave, and existing-ID collision checks |
[stderr] | F015 | Structural/traceability quality gate and recovery procedure | F014 output plus manifest | Passing ownership, metadata, dependency, asset, and browser-path checks |
[stderr] | F016 | Project README, registrations, evidence map, and resume handoff | F015 quality evidence | Explicit holds and safe resume points retained |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Within later implementation contracts, preserve the DM order: select and
[stderr] bootstrap the framework before core/state/content; establish persistence and
[stderr] recovery before session/UI; complete campaign rules before their UI; then input
[stderr] and accessibility, PWA lifecycle, release hardening, quality evidence, and
[stderr] documentation. Parallel authoring is allowed only for contracts whose declared
[stderr] dependencies are already satisfied and whose files/data/browser paths do not
[stderr] overlap.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Decision gates, approvals, and stop conditions
[stderr] 
[stderr] The following gates require a human or responsible external owner before a
[stderr] dependent contract is allocated as ready: engine/framework and package manager;
[stderr] CI runtime; browser storage API/quota; browser/device/assistive-tech and
[stderr] network matrix; numeric performance budget; host/CDN and service-worker rollout;
[stderr] content quantities, party size, balance, locale, audio and remapping scope;
[stderr] font/contrast targets; diagnostics provider/transport, retention and privacy;
[stderr] and ratings, age, regional, legal, support, store, or certification policy.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Stop the batch authoring system and mark the item blocked—not failed or
[stderr] implicitly approved—when any of these occurs:
[stderr] 
[stderr] - an open decision changes scope, privacy/security, cost, legal obligations,
[stderr]   connected services, or delivery commitments;
[stderr] - a requirement lacks a source, owner, verification approach, or safe bounded
[stderr]   contract; a dependency is cyclic or ownership conflicts;
[stderr] - an R/B/asset ID, output path, browser test path, or accepted contract would
[stderr]   be overwritten, renumbered, duplicated, or ambiguously owned;
[stderr] - a request entails credentials, paid services, account creation, store action,
[stderr]   deployment, publishing, release approval, or real-device/certification access;
[stderr] - evidence would require claiming that planned paths, generated runbooks, or
[stderr]   structural validation prove product implementation or release readiness.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Safe authoring-agent configuration
[stderr] 
[stderr] Use a workspace-scoped authoring agent with no secrets, deployments, payments,
[stderr] publishing, external account actions, or authority to approve gates. Give each
[stderr] agent only its manifest context packet, the source documents it cites, its
[stderr] assigned immutable IDs, and its declared output paths. It may create Markdown,
[stderr] JSON manifests, validation instructions, and child-registration files only in
[stderr] this project workspace; it must not create product source, generated game
[stderr] assets, tests that purport to validate a product, or release artefacts in this
[stderr] F011–F016 phase.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every authoring task records source links, requirement/contract IDs,
[stderr] dependencies, owned outputs, unresolved questions, gate status, and structural
[stderr] evidence. It checkpoints after each bounded batch, retains accepted IDs and
[stderr] manifest counters, and resumes from the next unaccepted item. F015 is the
[stderr] structural evidence gate: it validates metadata, traceability, dependency
[stderr] closure, output ownership, asset assessment, and browser-path uniqueness. Any
[stderr] later executable test, browser result, visual review, accessibility assessment,
[stderr] or release evidence must be stored at the paths named by its implementation
[stderr] contract and remains outside this authoring handoff.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Assumptions and risks carried forward
[stderr] 
[stderr] Safe assumptions are limited to an offline-first local campaign, original
[stderr] content/UI, shared core with necessary target adaptations, local durable saves
[stderr] where browser storage allows, visibly degraded connected features if ever
[stderr] approved, and privacy-minimised diagnostics. They are guardrails, not stack,
[stderr] vendor, backend, account, retention, performance, accessibility, or support
[stderr] promises.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Key risks are unresolved connected-scope conflict; storage/quota and update
[stderr] loss hazards; unsupported browser/device or assistive-tech assumptions;
[stderr] unapproved performance targets; original-design/provenance failure; and
[stderr] manifest/ID drift across resumed authoring waves. Mitigate each by retaining
[stderr] the named gate, keeping save/cache responsibilities separated, requiring
[stderr] provenance and human creative review, and using the F013 ledger plus F015
[stderr] quality gate before dispatching further work.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Readiness statement
[stderr] 
[stderr] The catalogue is ready for **batch authoring only**: audited inputs identify
[stderr] the P0 boundary, contract namespaces, delivery dependencies, evidence owners,
[stderr] and unresolved decision gates. F011–F016 may now formalise, shard, allocate,
[stderr] validate, and hand off implementation instructions. This statement does not
[stderr] select a technology, authorize any gate, create implementation or release
[stderr] work, or assert that the game has been built, tested, or approved for release.
[stderr] # Implementation catalogue index
[stderr] 
[stderr] This is the F012 implementation-authoring index for **mock-fantasy-quest-4**.
[stderr] The machine-readable source is
[stderr] [`implementation-catalogue-index.json`](implementation-catalogue-index.json).
[stderr] It decomposes only the first bounded shard; it does not author R-series
[stderr] runbooks or approve any decision gate.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Scope and ownership rules
[stderr] 
[stderr] - **SHARD-01 — Foundation and core contracts** contains 12 independently
[stderr]   verifiable contracts (limit: 250). It establishes framework-neutral seams,
[stderr]   deterministic data contracts, local persistence/recovery protocols, quality
[stderr]   controls, and documentation. It does not implement campaign behaviour,
[stderr]   player UI, PWA lifecycle, or release actions.
[stderr] - **Shared** owns campaign rules and platform-neutral contracts. **Web** owns
[stderr]   browser input, responsive layout, origin storage, and web builds. **PWA** is
[stderr]   a delivery/cache/install adaptation only: it reuses the shared game and web
[stderr]   UI, while owning its service-worker lifecycle. Save data and cache data are
[stderr]   deliberately separate.
[stderr] - `GATE-*` records block dependent authoring; they are not defaults. The
[stderr]   catalogue keeps connected capabilities out of P0 under `GATE-0006`.
[stderr] - Content must be original. The functional reference never authorises copied
[stderr]   names, text, lore, art, maps, layouts, music, or interaction choreography.
[stderr] 
