[stderr] +# Runbook-authoring allocation ledger
[stderr] +
[stderr] +This readable companion to [`runbook-authoring-manifest.json`](runbook-authoring-manifest.json) is the persistent allocation ledger for the mock-fantasy-quest-4 authoring system. The JSON is authoritative for IDs, provenance, dependencies, output paths, counters, and recovery state. Accepted IDs are immutable: resume from the first non-accepted batch and append only at `B0007` and `R0013`.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Wave 1 — foundation contracts
[stderr] +
[stderr] +| Order | Batch | R contracts (maximum 7) | Dependency / parallel disposition | Checkpoint |
[stderr] +| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] +| 1 | B0001 | R0001 | GATE-0001; no parallel work | Record gate and R0001 ownership |
[stderr] +| 2 | B0002 | R0002, R0003, R0010 | After B0001 | Validate seams, fixtures, tokens and retained gates |
[stderr] +| 3 | B0003 | R0004, R0008 | After B0002 | Validate state slice and P0 service exclusion |
[stderr] +| 4 | B0004 | R0005, R0006 | After B0003; R0006 waits for GATE-0003 | Validate content/save separation |
[stderr] +| 5 | B0005 | R0007, R0009, R0011 | After B0004; R0007/GATE-0003 and R0009/GATE-0005 remain blocked | Validate migration, diagnostics, provenance |
[stderr] +| 6 | B0006 | R0012 | After B0003 and B0004; safe in parallel with B0005 after B0004 | Cross-check foundation documentation and gates |
[stderr] +
[stderr] +The batches are deliberately smaller than seven where a contract is context-heavy (state, persistence, content) or tightly coupled to its predecessor. Together they form an incremental foundation vertical slice: reproducible baseline, shared seams/test harness, deterministic game state and P0 exclusion, content and recovery boundaries, then provenance/diagnostic controls and documented closure. They do not imply that the game is implemented.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Contract allocation and asset assessment
[stderr] +
[stderr] +| R ID | IMP source | Writer | Output | Asset assessment |
[stderr] +| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] +| R0001 | IMP-0001 | B0001 | `runbooks/R0001.md` | not applicable — tooling boundary only |
[stderr] +| R0002 | IMP-0002 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0002.md` | not applicable — interfaces only |
[stderr] +| R0003 | IMP-0003 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0003.md` | not applicable — deterministic fixtures only |
[stderr] +| R0004 | IMP-0004 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0004.md` | not applicable — state envelopes only |
[stderr] +| R0005 | IMP-0005 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0005.md` | not applicable — schema reserves later asset fields |
[stderr] +| R0006 | IMP-0006 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0006.md` | not applicable — save/recovery protocol |
[stderr] +| R0007 | IMP-0007 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0007.md` | not applicable — migrations only |
[stderr] +| R0008 | IMP-0008 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0008.md` | not applicable — negative service scope |
[stderr] +| R0009 | IMP-0009 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0009.md` | not applicable — local diagnostics only |
[stderr] +| R0010 | IMP-0010 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0010.md` | not applicable — no final art/fonts/icons |
[stderr] +| R0011 | IMP-0011 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0011.md` | not applicable — provenance control only |
[stderr] +| R0012 | IMP-0012 | B0006 | `runbooks/R0012.md` | not applicable — documentation only |
[stderr] +
[stderr] +No Wave 1 contract creates a product asset. Each contract still carries an explicit `asset_impact`, empty `asset_ids`, and an asset brief in the JSON; its author must prohibit incidental asset creation. A later asset-required contract must reserve stable product-specific IDs and a brief before assignment.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Author packet and resume rules
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Each B writer receives only the baseline terminology, target constraints, templates, authoring rules, and the named specification/catalogue records in its JSON `context_packet`. Every R record has canonical `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`, and `factory_stages` provenance. Use [`runbooks/TEMPLATE.md`](../../../runbooks/TEMPLATE.md) plus the F014 metadata and asset requirements.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +At every checkpoint, validate unique B/R IDs, output-path ownership, dependency closure, provenance, the batch maximum, asset assessment, and retained human gates. On failure, retain all assigned IDs, repair the bounded failed output, and resume that same batch. Never regenerate accepted contracts or renumber the ledger.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Queued expansion chapters
[stderr] +
[stderr] +| Chapter | Future allocation point | State |
[stderr] +| --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] +| SHARD-02 — Campaign content and domain logic | Wave 2 after GATE-0004 | queued |
[stderr] +| SHARD-03 — Session, UI, input, and accessibility | Wave 3 after SHARD-02 and GATE-0002/0003/0004 | queued |
[stderr] +| SHARD-04 — PWA delivery, performance, operations, release evidence | Wave 4 after SHARD-02/03 and GATE-0002/0005/0007 | queued |
[stderr] +| SHARD-05 — Connected-scope decision package | Only explicit authorisation after GATE-0006; no P0 R allocation | hold |
[stderr] +
[stderr] +Expand one queued chapter into catalogue records before allocating its R IDs. This preserves the catalogue’s dependency graph and makes a future thousand-runbook programme resumable without changing accepted work.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +## Structural check
[stderr] +
[stderr] +```sh
[stderr] +node -e 'const m=require("./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json"); const r=m.r_contracts, b=m.batches, ids=a=>new Set(a).size===a.length; if (!ids(r.map(x=>x.id)) || !ids(b.map(x=>x.id)) || r.length!==m.expected_runbook_count || b.some(x=>x.r_contract_ids.length>7) || r.some(x=>!x.source_specifications.length || !x.source_catalogue_ids.length || !x.authoring_batch || !x.factory_stages.length || !["required","not_applicable"].includes(x.asset_impact) || !x.output_path || (x.asset_impact==="required" && (!x.asset_ids.length || !x.asset_brief))) ) process.exit(1); console.log("runbook authoring ledger structural check passed")'
[stderr] +```
